side in production systems,

157
00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:07.030
it's not uncommon to see a
beam width maybe around 10.

158
00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:09.700
I think a beam width
of 100 would be

159
00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:13.150
considered very large
for a production system,

160
00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:15.255
depending on the application.

161
00:08:15.255 --> 00:08:18.625
But for research systems where
people want to squeeze out

162
00:08:18.625 --> 00:08:20.680
every last drop of
performance in order

163
00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:22.710
to publish a paper with the
best possible result,

164
00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:25.105
it's not uncommon
to see people use

165
00:08:25.105 --> 00:08:28.055
beam width of 1,000 or 3,000,

166
00:08:28.055 --> 00:08:30.775
but this is very application

167
00:08:30.775 --> 00:08:33.410
as well as a domain dependent.

168
00:08:33.410 --> 00:08:36.310
I would say try out a
variety of values of

169
00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:39.355
beam as see what works
for your application,

170
00:08:39.355 --> 00:08:41.775
but when beam is very large,

171
00:08:41.775 --> 00:08:45.365
there is often
diminishing returns.

172
00:08:45.365 --> 00:08:49.030
For many applications,
I would expect to see

173
00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:51.970
a huge gain as you
go from beam of one,

174
00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.730
which is basically greedy search
to three to maybe 10,

175
00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:57.490
but the gains as you
go from the thousands

176
00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:01.445
to three thousand beam width
might not be as big.

177
00:09:01.445 --> 00:09:03.970
For those of you that have

